# xLaDe: exp-005

This past week, we tried an experiment on xLaDe. 
It was exp-005 based on https://github.com/BaDaaS/lean4-courses and it was not like other experiments. 
The reason was that we ourselves had created exp-001 to exp-004 but the exp-005 wasn’t created by xLaDe team, it was created by Danny Willems (https://github.com/dannywillems) and I would like to thank him, but the unique thing was — he had no idea about the xLaDe project. 
In fact, his project had no code relation or mention of xLaDe. 
So, both social connections wise and programming wise, the project and its owner were not familiar with us. 
This was the first time we were doing something like this.

## Why only this project and not any other?

Well, it was not my first choice project for exp-005. 
I first tried pfr project of Terence Tao (https://github.com/teorth/pfr) and the reason was obviously Terry. 
I even started working on it, started by cloning repo locally via git, and trying to built. 
Then I tried to do lake build. 
Both the good and bad thing happened. 
Good thing — It didn’t had mathlib as dependency. Bad thing — It had mathlib as dependency.

For context, I live in himalayan region, so the internet connection is not good and large downloads either take hours or simply self cancel due to internet problems. 
When I first downloaded cs, it took me like a week (but with pauses though).

So, back to pfr, it had two dependencies, namely — AddCombi (https://github.com/YaelDillies/add-combi.git) and checkdecls (https://github.com/PatrickMassot/checkdecls.git). 
Since both were small, I was content. 
Suddenly, I saw mathlib getting downloaded. 
Shocked I saw, and then found, it was add-combi’s dependency. 
I thought, “The hardest part about creating a perpetual motion machine is figuring out where to hide the battery and the hardest part about creating a Lean4 project is figuring out where to hide the mathlib.”

Then, I abandoned it after cancelling and deleted the repository. 
Then, I begun looking for simpler projects. 
I squinted at Terry’s repos but accepted defeat. 
Then I remembered I had starred a Lean4 project months ago — Lean4 courses by BaDaaS aka Denny Willems. 
I wanted to learn Lean4 so I had starred it, but due to lack of time and laziness, I couldn’t start it and I had partially forgotten about the project. 
So, I checked the dependecies first, but none were there. 
It was just a simple course for learning Lean4, so I thought of making this as the exp-005

## Creation:

We created exp-005/ inside experiments/ module, and then added lean4-courses as a submodule. 
Then I wrote README.md, experiment.toml and METRICS.md and this is the toml file:

```
id = "EXP-005"
name = "Lean4 Courses"
type = "script-policy"
status = "active"
allowed_modes = ["experimental"]
lean_toolchain = "leanprover/lean4:v4.30.0"
entry = "scripts/experiments/run-exp-005.sh"
description = "External Lean 4 course repository by BaDaaS. 32 modules covering foundations to advanced metaprogramming. Zero dependencies beyond stdlib. First external project xLaDe did not author and has no code connection to."
success_criteria = "compilation"
project_repo = "https://github.com/BaDaaS/lean4-courses"
project_submodule = "experiments/exp-005-lean4-courses/lean4-courses"
```

However, the problem was that, I had lean4 v4.30 locally and it required v4.29 for build. 
As we already know, due to unreliable internet I couldn’t download previous version. 
So, for temporary workaround and testing, I decided to do it with my local lean4 version. 
I replaced v4.29 with v4.30 and completed the building process. 
Next, I created script file for the exp-005 which is available in the scripts/ module. 
For complete files and scripts, please check the xLaDe repository at any git hositing from GitHub, Gitlab, Codeberg, Bitbucket and Gitea.

