## Limitations:

We notice the following limitations for our exp-005 and hope documenting it will improve the project in future.

1. It was a simple course with elementary Lean without any external dependency so we need to improve xLaDe for that types of projects too. 
It would be interesting to see projects with dependencies hell running inside xLaDe.

2. exp-005 was run on v4.30 instead of v4.29 however we plan to run it on v4.29 soon enough.

3. The manual and tedious process for each experiment needs to be optimised for the real world usage or as a serious research tool. Other projects’ maintainers won’t try to optimise their project or debug if the projects’ fail. However, this is a future problem and I am not thinking about this problem to be honest.

## Final Words:

We kept our promise — xLaDe works on the ecosystem level and we interfare neither with Lean4 kernel nor external projects. 
I didn’t add xLaDe code to the lean4-courses repo, I didn’t add reference of xLaDe in it, I didn’t change the code. 
Even the repository owner doesn’t know we performed this experiment I guess. 
But we succeeded. 
We don’t want xLaDe to be performative tool, we want it to be like a unix tool — do a single task, and do it properly. 
Hopefully, we will add some more experiments this release cycle as mentioned in our roadmap and will get new results. 
I am happy to see the progress although I had expected this to fail and even documented it in roadmap already.

Weird coincidence — just two hours ago, they added mathlab dependency on pfr repository directly.

With that said, I am happy to collaborate with others and get suggestions on which experiments we should try next. 
However, due to problem with resources, internet and infrastructure, I won’t be able to do ‘huge experiments’, but I hope to restart them probably after 1.5 months or similar.

